[Music]  the war in ukraine shows it is almost  impossible to live a normal life when  fighting is going all around you think  about that for just a second more than  four million people have fled from the  country since russia invaded five weeks  ago but ninety percent  ninety percent of the country's  population has stayed trying to do their  work and support their families deborah  pat is in the capitol keefe she spoke to  some of the people who have found a way  to function in the chaos all around them  when your home is being shelled and your  town besieged you run as far away as you  can people have fled the worst of the  war to the relative safety of cave and  surrounding towns  in bravery katja found shelter in this  kindergarten with her four children  after her home burnt down now she shares  a classroom with over 25 other people  are you worried that the russians could  come here  of course we're worried about it she  says  nowhere is completely safe in the  capital where air sirens and distant  shelling have become the soundtrack of  daily life the streets have emptied out  this is the heart of the city it used to  be a vibrant center now it's a fortified  military zone  before the wall victor was an  electrician he last picked up a gun  during his mandatory conscription 12  years ago  at the beginning i was astounded at  what's happening he said then i realized  it's not a joke it's actually a  patriotic wall  incredibly grocery stores are still open  and when there is no physical comfort to  be found  there is always the spiritual  prayers for peace have never been more  urgent  [Music]  in underground bars turned bunkers young  volunteers relax and tell war stories  outside others mix cocktails the molotov  kind instead of partying the night away  they also provide food and shelter for  exhausted fighters  max palienko walks to work every morning  to open up his basement flower shop  instead of a gun he's holding out a hand  of friendship selling flowers and hope  it's my job  brilliant smile and happiness  i'm staying in the cave  katja and her kids are here to stay too  they have nowhere else to go  are you scared  we are she says are you scared of the  alarms and when these big bangs  no  lives turned upside down by war  for cbs morning's debra patter cave  ukraine  life turned upside down but carries on

